Twin flames crackle in low braziers in a small, square room that feels
strangely safe after the halls below. The stone between them is polished by
long vigil, and above them a sentence is carved twice, as though its writer
feared one copy would not be enough: DIGDOGGER HATES CERTAIN KIND OF SOUND.
The only way out is the iron-banded door with a keyhole in the south wall.
